\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces (a) Confusion matrix of v1.1.0 (rows: true; columns: predicted), from \nolinkurl  {models/scoliosis_classifier_v1.1.0/evaluation_results.json}. (b) Output of \texttt  {python ai/model\_probe.py -{}-version 1.1.0} (\nolinkurl  {probe_results.json}) on eight synthetic, very different inputs: the probabilities change with the image (trimmed range 0.89 against the 0.10 required). The same probe on v1.0.0 gives a trimmed range of 0.009: the probability of every class barely moves, which is how a model that ignores its input is detected without any dataset. The probe images are synthetic and are \emph  {not} a clinical test.}}{34}{figure.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:confusion}{{13}{34}{(a) Confusion matrix of v1.1.0 (rows: true; columns: predicted), from \fp {models/scoliosis_classifier_v1.1.0/evaluation_results.json}. (b) Output of \texttt {python ai/model\_probe.py -{}-version 1.1.0} (\fp {probe_results.json}) on eight synthetic, very different inputs: the probabilities change with the image (trimmed range 0.89 against the 0.10 required). The same probe on v1.0.0 gives a trimmed range of 0.009: the probability of every class barely moves, which is how a model that ignores its input is detected without any dataset. The probe images are synthetic and are \emph {not} a clinical test}{figure.caption.29}{}}
